TDM (The Disintegrated Machine) simulator
=========================================

An instruction set simulator for TDM, following the RTL model of the TDM
project (rtl/cpu.py): 5 clock cycles per instruction, 6 for SWAP, every
memory operand read and then written back.  It runs ELF programs from
their entry point (the reset vector at MEM[0] isn't used):

  tdm-elf-run [--trace-disasm] program

The CPU model includes the parts that are on the CPU in the hardware:

  0xfffe  interrupt controller
          read:  enable mask in bits 15..8, pending sources in 7..0
          write: bits 15..8 set the enable mask, a 1 in bits 7..0
                 acknowledges that source
          Source 0 is the MMU's access violation, 1..7 are external.
  0xffff  MMU control register (write only; reads see 0xffff)
          offset in bits 15..9, limit in 8..2 (in 512-word pages, the
          limit is inclusive), user mode in 1; a 1 in bit 0 clears the
          access violation.

BRK (0b0xxx_1_111_xxxxxxxx, see "brk" in the assembler) stops the
simulation with SIGTRAP, with the PC on the BRK; GDB uses 0x1f00 for
its breakpoints.  The other opcode 4 words stop it with SIGILL.

Besides pc, sp, r0 and r1, the debugger can read and write the CPU
state that software can't see (sim/sim-tdm.h): intdis, the MMU fields
and access violation bit, the interrupt enable and pending bits, and
the cycle count.  The debugger accesses physical memory.

Everything else goes to the bus.  By default there's 16K words of memory
(0x0000..0x3fff) and the tdm_stdio device at 0xffe0..0xffe2:

  0xffe0  EXIT    write: stop, the value is the exit status
  0xffe1  STDOUT  write: print the low 8 bits
  0xffe2  STDIN   read: the next character, 0xffff at end of file

Writes with bit 15 set are ignored and reads return 0x8000 (except for
STDIN), so the write-back after a read does nothing.  Accessing an
address nothing decodes stops the simulation with SIGSEGV.

Devices are configured through the hw device tree, and --hw-device
settings take precedence over the defaults.  The sim core is octet
addressed: addresses and sizes given to --hw-device and the --memory-*
options are twice the TDM word address.

  --memory-region 0,0x20000                    64K words of memory
  --hw-device "/core/tdm_stdio/reg 0x1ffd0 6"  stdio device at 0xffe8
  --hw-device "/core/tdm_stdio/disabled true"  no stdio device

External interrupt lines are the input ports int1..int7 of /tdm_cpu:

  --hw-device "/some/device > irq int3 /tdm_cpu"
